<h2>Start with what customers need to do</h2>
<p>Choosing website features feels harder than it should — every platform advertises booking, chat, and full stores on day one. This guide separates <strong>what your business needs customers to do</strong> (call, book, buy) from <strong>what Webstar can ship in each package</strong>. For line-item pricing, see the <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/website-cost-small-business-michigan-2026/')); ?>">2026 cost guide</a>; for tier details, see <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('packages/')); ?>">packages</a>.</p>

<h2>1. How should customers reach you?</h2>
<p>The default for most Michigan service businesses is still correct: <strong>phone, contact form, and tap-to-call on mobile</strong>. That pattern matches how owners actually want leads — a human conversation, not a maze of widgets.</p>
<p>We can add SMS-friendly contact paths or lightweight live messaging when it fits your workflow, usually as a scoped add-on on top of call and form. If your team lives in email, keep the form short; if you answer the phone, make the number impossible to miss on every page.</p>

<h2>2. Brochure features (the What and How)</h2>
<p>Home, services, contact, and trust pages answer two questions: <strong>what you do</strong> and <strong>how you deliver it</strong>. Service businesses and shops both need that clarity; ecommerce adds product and checkout paths on top of the same trust layer.</p>
<p>On a tight scope, three pages are enough — see <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/what-to-put-on-3-page-website/')); ?>">what to put on a 3-page site</a>. When you need separate commercial pages, galleries, or staff bios, you are usually past brochure-only scope and into <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('packages/business-website/')); ?>">Business Website</a> territory.</p>

<h2>3. Booking and scheduling</h2>
<p>Booking tools plug in at the Business tier and above — one integration is included on Business Website; Growth supports multiple flows and custom rules.</p>
<p>Free or low-cost schedulers (Google Calendar plus payment links, Calendly) work for solo operators. Paid platforms (FareHarbor for tours, Square Appointments for staff schedules) make sense when complexity justifies the subscription. We wire the tool you already use; we do not force a single vendor on every industry.</p>
<p>If you only need a “Book now” button that dials the shop, <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('packages/simple-website/')); ?>">Simple Website</a> is often enough — real calendar embeds usually mean Business scope.</p>

<h2>4. E-commerce and inventory</h2>
<p>Modern sites rarely need custom cart code from scratch. <strong>APIs</strong> connect your branded front end to inventory, tax, shipping, and payments that already exist elsewhere.</p>
<p>For many small businesses, <strong>Square’s free tier</strong> is the practical default: custom site design on Webstar’s side, checkout and card security on Square’s. That is how we approach low-cost custom stores without reinventing PCI compliance.</p>
<p>Shopify, BigCommerce, Lightspeed, and WooCommerce trade extra monthly cost for polished admin UIs — inventory rules, discounts, shipping zones, and order queues in one dashboard. Paying roughly $40/month for Shopify or BigCommerce is reasonable when you live in that backend daily and want less custom glue.</p>
<p>Heavy catalog logic, multi-location inventory, or non-Square workflows belong in a written <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('packages/business-growth-website/')); ?>">Business Growth</a> estimate — scope varies too much for a flat brochure price.</p>

<h2>5. Features you can add later</h2>
<p>All-in-one builders often mean rebuild when you outgrow the template. With Webstar, the base site is custom HTML/PHP you own; new integrations are quoted <strong>à la carte</strong> — booking embeds, Square catalog sync, extra integrations, new page sets — instead of throwing away the whole site.</p>
<p>Routine hosting, backups, and small edits can stay on a <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('plans/')); ?>">Care Plan</a> or <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/website-as-a-service-vs-buy-outright/')); ?>">Website as a Service</a>; one-off integration work is billed per job so you are not locked into features you do not need yet.</p>
<p>Example path: launch on Simple with call and form, add Calendly when you hire a second chair, add Square when you start shipping tackle — each step is a defined change order, not a platform migration.</p>

